From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bharat Dintakurti <bdintakurti@nvidia.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 17:18:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] NVIDIA: SAUCE: ACPI: processor_idle: Serialize and recover
 LPI topology updates

BugLink: https://bugs.launchpad.net/bugs/2167301

CPU-scoped drivers and generated LPI domains turn an _LPI notification or
processor removal into a whole-topology operation. The existing CPU0-only
state refresh can race processor startup and can leave driver or domain
objects referring to removed data.

Introduce BUILDING, DIRECT, and UPDATING lifecycle states for the
still-direct hierarchical implementation. Preserve the existing shared
driver notification, hot-add, failure, and teardown paths for legacy _CST
and flat LPI systems. Carry the shared rebuild mutex across both flat and
hierarchical rebuilds.

ACPI notifications run on kacpi_notify_wq, which system suspend drains
while holding the system-transition lock. Do not rebuild or wait for that
lock in the notification callback. Queue independent delayed work instead,
retain a generation while replacement is in progress, and coalesce
notifications without losing a request.

A request from any represented topology CPU now excludes system sleep and
CPU removal, releases the old hierarchy, rebuilds every per-CPU driver and
device, and validates the replacement before reporting success. Keep shared
entry blocked and retry reconstruction failures after destructive teardown.
Preserve extraction failures that require retry instead of silently
converting them to flat fallback.

Processor removal can clear the per-CPU processor pointer before deferred
or global cleanup. Key teardown by CPU ID and make the processor object
optional where only its flags need updating. Visit excluded CPUs as well,
because exclusion controls activation eligibility rather than resource
ownership. Keep topology mode sticky after CPU-scoped setup so recovery
continues through the same lifecycle.

Remove and drain a processor's ACPI notification handler before releasing
idle resources. Mark first-time CPU hot-add pending before start publishes
the CPU, queue replacement after start completes, and cancel delayed work
at driver exit. Serialize final teardown with the rebuild transaction and
lifecycle locks, taking the rebuild mutex outside the system-sleep lock so
the ordering remains effective when CONFIG_PM_SLEEP is disabled. PSCI OSI
is not enabled by this change.

